+The record **types** of the multipart commit protocol (proposal 0016, still a draft) are landed ahead of most of their writers, under disjoint key prefixes: the `mpuctl` admission ledger singleton (a `Budget` profile and the session count it derives `MAX_SESSIONS` from), and per-upload `mpu:<id>` (a session's target, lifecycle state and epoch, and its segment-group nonce — stored in every state from Create to the terminal delete, so the batch that ends a `Completing` attempt can name that attempt's segment records, the nonce at the attempt's fence epoch, and the terminal delete can name the group's reservation marker even for an upload that never reached Complete), `slot:<id>:<k>` (an in-flight part reservation), `part:<id>:<n>` (a committed part's chunk list, length and digest) and `psum:<id>:<n>` (that part's summary), and the reclamation pair `retire:bytes:<token>` (bytes to orphan-mark, then the records that named them) and `retire:records:<token>` (records to delete whose bytes something else still protects), whose value names what one obligation owes — a session's staged residue, a range-encoded set of its part numbers, one part attempt's chunk list, a superseded generation (by the chunk list a flat root carried, or by the segment group a segmented one named — the two shapes a published map has, never both) or a rolled-back completion attempt's segment records, in the combinations the protocol installs as single obligations — every one validated structurally at decode (ADR-0045) rather than trusted on successful JSON parsing alone, so a stored record whose fields disagree with each other (a session whose publish target names another object or another fence epoch, a part whose length is not its chunks' summed length, a slot born already lapsed) is a typed error rather than a live value. A retirement obligation's identity lives partly in its `<token>` — which session at which fence epoch, which part attempt of it, which superseded generation — so its value is decoded **against its own key**, and a payload whose mode, token scope, generation identity or segment epoch disagrees with the token naming it is likewise a typed error rather than an obligation that would reclaim one attempt's bytes while clearing another's. The owned staging entry `sidx:<id>:<n>:<chunk>` is the lease record a streaming write stores under `pending:<chunk>`, plus the owning upload id (`owner`) and the chunk's planned placement (`staged` — an erasure scheme the coder must support, and a D server per planned fragment, a count that decode leaves unchecked as for a committed chunk). Both are present on an owned entry and absent on an ordinary lease, and each is omitted when absent, so a `pending:` record written before them decodes and re-encodes byte-identically — load-bearing because a renewal puts a freshly encoded entry over the bytes it read. A value whose fields disagree with each other or with its key (one field alone, an owned entry under `pending:`, an ordinary lease under `sidx:`, an owner other than the key's) is refused by every reader and both `pending:` writers, and an expiry sweep skips it without reclaiming its chunk; GC's expired-lease input, off unless an operator arms it, also names it on the audit seam, and the post-restore reconciliation holds its chunk unmarked and reports the entry as needing a human, as it does an unreadable chunk map. An ownership field spelled `null` is refused too: the encoder never emits that spelling, so a lease that carried it would be rewritten by its next renewal. Their first production reader and writer is the post-restore session fence (`wyrd custodian --reconcile-after-restore`, `crates/custodian/src/restore.rs`): after its reference passes it re-lists `mpu:` in bounded pages and moves each `Open@E` session to `Aborting@E+1` in one commit with its `retire:` `{session, all}` obligation, so a session resurrected by the restore cannot be completed over reclaimed bytes (proposal 0016 D-B). The first client-driven writers, Create and Abort, are in `wyrd-core` (`multipart::store`, #656); no production path calls them until the S3 verbs arrive (#668). Create admits a session in one commit. That commit requires the bucket marker's exact bytes, the `mpuctl` ledger's exact bytes (or, on a store that has none, its absence: the first Create installs the ledger from its own profile, so there is no migration step), and the absence of the new upload id's `mpu:` key and the new nonce's `seggrp:` key. It writes the `Open` session at epoch 0, the reservation marker, and the ledger at `count + 1`. A create is refused with nothing written when the ledger is at its stored limit, when the caller's profile differs from the stored one, or when the bucket is absent or its marker moved before the commit. Before reading anything it also refuses a session too large to fit, in its widest later state, under the backend value ceiling (`Completing` names the object a second time): every later transition rewrites the whole record, so a session that fits only as `Open` could be admitted but never fenced or torn down. A create that loses the ledger's compare-and-set to another, or whose commit's outcome the store could not report, backs off and retries under the same identity, up to as many times as its profile's session limit. So a refusal for contention never answers ordinary concurrency on a ledger with room: it means that, that many times over, either the ledger moved under the create or the store could not report a commit's outcome. Only a collision on the id or the nonce mints a new identity, so the two retry budgets never share a count. Create reads both keys of an identity before its first commit, so a collision with a session that already exists is found before anything is in doubt. A commit whose outcome the store cannot report is re-driven under the same upload id with the ledger condition read afresh, and a create about to end while such a commit may have landed looks for its session once more. It takes a session found there for its own only while one of its commits is in doubt and only when every field the create wrote matches. So a create is admitted at most once, never under a second id and never under another upload's, and one whose commit landed is answered as created. Abort fences an `Open` session to `Aborting` with its `{session, all}` obligation in the same commit shape the post-restore fence uses (one batch builder serves both), answers an already-`Aborting` session as done, and refuses a `Completing`, `Completed` or unknown session with nothing written. It never changes the admission count, whose only decrement is the terminal delete. The rest of the protocol (UploadPart, Complete's fenced transitions and staged publication, the retirement drain and the terminal delete) arrives with #657–#659 and is specified in the proposal, not here. Because every transition it specifies is a compare-and-set on the record's exact bytes, the decoders enforce serialization identity now, before the protocol's writers exist to depend on it: the accepted set is exactly the encoder's image, byte for byte — an absent `content_type` is spelled only by omitting the field (the inode trio's omitted-when-absent rule), a noncanonical spelling of an accepted value (fields reordered, whitespace inserted) is a decode error rather than bytes a CAS could never match, and a part record's chunk values are read through a closed wire shape of their own, so an unknown or omitted chunk field is a decode error rather than a silent rewrite.
 
 The post-restore reconciliation keeps one more singleton beside them, `mpufence`: the **restore-fence generation** that proposal 0016 requires to complete before any gateway serves multipart verbs on a restored image (0016 names the generation but gives it no key). Its value is `{"generation":N,"complete":B}`, closed and canonical like `mpuctl`'s. `generation` numbers that pass's runs from 1, so the record is absent until the first run, and absent is the only spelling of "no run has happened". `complete` says whether that run finished its session fence. Each run first writes the next generation as not complete, and marks or fences nothing until that write is acknowledged. After its last write it records the generation complete, but only if every write it made was acknowledged and no upload session is left for a human: one it could not read or fence, or a fenced one whose segment records need a human. Every run judges those sessions afresh from their records, so a session an earlier run fenced but could not clear holds back every later run until someone repairs it. Findings that are not about a session, such as a lost or misplaced chunk or an unreadable object or staged record, do not hold the generation back. Both writes are conditioned on the bytes last read there, so a completion that lands late never overwrites a newer run's generation. What the record cannot tell: a restore rewinds it with everything else, so an image captured after an earlier run completed comes back reading complete until the next run starts. No gateway reads it yet. The gateway gate (#508) needs a restore-scoped signal beside it, and until then what keeps multipart off a restored image is the runbook's order: the reconciliation runs with writers stopped, before any gateway is re-enabled.
 
